# The cpu microcode loaded next to the kernel. The kernel itself is NOT here:
# it is the one part of the core that is properly per-distro - a desktop and an
# appliance want different drivers built in, different console behaviour and
# different boot times. Each distro names the kernel package it wants in its own
# packages.list. This list is concatenated with the distro's, so anything left
# here would be installed alongside whatever the distro chose.
12-make-cpio.tar.gz
3-make-microcode.tar.gz

# The package manager. It is in the core because it costs the core nothing:
# every command it runs - bash, coreutils, grep, sed, gawk, findutils, tar,
# util-linux and openssl - is already installed above for other reasons, so
# this package is the 40 KB of shell itself. It brings /usr/bin/lpkg, the
# soname scanner it shares with the build host, and the file-policy table it
# resolves shared files with; the channel URL and the trusted key are written
# by build-distro.sh, being properties of an installation rather than of a
# package.
#
# curl, so lpkg can read a channel over http. Without it a REPO_URL has to be
# a path or a file:// URL - removable media, an NFS mount - which works but is
# not what most installations want. Its whole soname closure was already here,
# so it costs 4 MB and pulls in nothing new.
17-make-curl.tar.gz

# binutils, for readelf: 'lpkg build' reads the sonames out of what it just
# compiled, and without it a locally built package records that it provides
# and needs nothing, which resolves against nothing.
#
# This is the expensive entry in the list. binutils links against libstdc++
# and libgcc_s, which live in the gcc package, so gcc and flex come with it:
# 398 MB stripped, on a core that was 681 MB. It buys one program.
#
# Two ways to make it cheaper, neither done here. Splitting libstdc++ and
# libgcc_s out of gcc into a gcc-libs package - which is what other
# distributions do - would bring this to about 57 MB, but the build has no way
# to say "this recipe writes these files and does not ship them". Or lpkg
# could run the scan inside the build chroot, where binutils is already part
# of the build root, and cost nothing at all.
# gmp, mpfr and mpc are here for two independent reasons, and missing one of
# them broke the other for a long time. gcc needs all three. gawk needs libgmp
# and libmpfr for its arbitrary precision arithmetic - which meant /usr/bin/awk
# could not start in an image that left mpfr out, and nothing that runs awk
# worked, lpkg included. 'make check' had been reporting it; deps-check had
# not, because minimal's deps.ignore excluded mpfr for an unrelated reason.
8.21-make-gmp.tar.gz
8.22-make-mpfr.tar.gz
8.23-make-mpc.tar.gz
8.15-make-flex.tar.gz
8.29-make-gcc.tar.gz
8.20-make-binutils.tar.gz

# Last, so that the file-policy.conf it ships is not overwritten by anything.
x-make-lpkg.tar.gz
